-Light Touch sends anonymous usage and error reports to TelemetryDeck (https://telemetrydeck.com): that it opened and how many devices of which kinds it has; which device starts, with its iOS version, how it was prepared and how long it took; the apps you open on a device and install in it, by their App Store identifiers; features such as screenshots, recordings and Play on Phone; and what failed, such as a device that didn’t start or stopped responding, a preparation, download, install or update that failed, storage the device couldn’t write, a graphics call an app made that the device couldn’t carry out (the call’s name and numbers, once per app each time the device starts), or a crash of Light Touch or one of its own processes, with the last steps before it. Each report includes the Mac’s model, graphics, macOS version and language, and the bezel and renderer settings. They carry no names, file paths or device identifiers, and no messages but the line iOS printed for a problem inside a device; a crash is sent only as where in Light Touch’s code it happened, and a random identifier for this copy of Light Touch is hashed before it’s sent. Turn off Light Touch → Settings → General → Share anonymous usage and error reports to stop them.
+Light Touch sends anonymous usage and error reports to TelemetryDeck (https://telemetrydeck.com): that it opened and how many devices of which kinds it has; which device starts, with its iOS version, how it was prepared and how long it took; the apps you open on a device and install in it, by their App Store identifiers; features such as screenshots, recordings and Play on Phone; and what failed, such as a device that didn’t start or stopped responding, a preparation, download, install or update that failed, storage the device couldn’t write, a graphics call an app made that the device couldn’t carry out (the call’s name and numbers, once per app each time the device starts), or a crash of Light Touch or one of its own processes, with the last steps before it. Each report includes the Mac’s model, graphics, macOS version and language, and the bezel and renderer settings. They carry no names, file paths or device identifiers, and no messages but the line iOS printed for a problem inside a device; a crash is sent only as where in Light Touch’s code it happened, and a random identifier for this copy of Light Touch is hashed before it’s sent. This Big Sur build sends them only once you turn on Light Touch → Settings → General → Share anonymous usage and error reports; turn it off to stop them.
 
